cff-version: 1.2.0
message: "If you use Lixity, please cite it as below."
title: "Lixity — Quantitative text linguistics, stylometry and self-calibrating style references"
version: "2.1.0"
date-released: "2026-10-08"
license: LicenseRef-Lixity-Non-Commercial-License-1.0
repository-code: "https://github.com/mfahsold/lixity"
url: "https://mfahsold.github.io/lixity/"
abstract: >-
  Offline, deterministic text-linguistics engine for literary manuscripts:
  sentence rhythm, lexical diversity (HD-D, MTLD, MATTR,
  Maas, Yule's K), language-specific readability, dialogue share,
  paragraph-accurate tense profiles, and a self-calibrating style reference
  (robust median/MAD bands, noise-adjusted z*, nominal Benjamini-Hochberg /
  Benjamini-Yekutieli selections, effect sizes) with exploratory diagnostics
  (guarded changepoints, Mann-Kendall, Sn/Qn, Hill, Wasserstein-KS,
  Dunning keyness and fitted co-occurrence degree tails). Pure Python 3.10+,
  no cloud calls. Statistical signals support human review, not quality scores.
authors:
  - family-names: Fahsold
    given-names: Matthias
    email: mfahsold@googlemail.com
keywords:
  - computational linguistics
  - stylometry
  - text analysis
  - literary manuscripts
  - tense profiling
  - style reference
  - lexical diversity
  - readability
  - digital humanities
  - macro-editing
